{
  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "We evaluated the models using the Mean Opinion Score (MOS) metric, which is widely used within the TTS domain."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "This assessment involved a survey conducted with native speakers to gauge the models performance based on different criteria."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "In an effort to minimize bias, we randomly selected a set of 200 utterances narrated by speakers. These utterances formed our “testing set,” deliberately excluded from the model training process."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "Following the completion of training, we utilized the TTS models to generate voice outputs for these utterances, thereby providing both machine-generated and human narrations for comparative analysis."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "To conduct the evaluation process, we developed a Telegram bot, as shown in Fig. 1, and shared the bot’s link with native Tatar speakers."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "This bot facilitated the assessment of 30 recordings per session, focusing on qualitative criteria such as audio quality, pronunciation, naturalness, and overall comprehensibility."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "Participants were requested to rate each criterion on a scale from 1 (bad) to 5 (excellent)."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "They were allowed to listen to the recordings multiple times but were restricted to submitting a single grade for each criterion."
    },
    {
      "ref": 4,
      "quote": "It is worth noting that the user interface of the bot was in Russian. This decision was influenced by the prevalent Tatar-Russian bilingualism observed within the target audience of the bot, as highlighted in the work of Wigglesworth-Baker [25]."
    }
  ],
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The evaluation showed that the models were assessed using the Mean Opinion Score (MOS) metric, which is widely used within the TTS domain [4]. A survey was conducted with native speakers to gauge the models' performance based on different criteria [4]. A set of 200 utterances narrated by speakers was randomly selected and formed the testing set, which was excluded from the model training process [4]. The TTS models were used to generate voice outputs for these utterances, providing both machine-generated and human narrations for comparative analysis [4]. A Telegram bot was developed to facilitate the assessment of 30 recordings per session, focusing on qualitative criteria such as audio quality, pronunciation, naturalness, and overall comprehensibility [4]. Participants were requested to rate each criterion on a scale from 1 (bad) to 5 (excellent) [4]. They were allowed to listen to the recordings multiple times but were restricted to submitting a single grade for each criterion [4]. The user interface of the bot was in Russian, influenced by the prevalent Tatar-Russian bilingualism observed within the target audience [4].",
  "clarification": ""
}

